/-!
# Minimal primes

Root import re-exporting the principal-ideal classification, its normalized-factor
equivalence, the associated-prime comparison for principal quotients, and the
equivalence with order-dual irreducible components of the prime-spectrum zero-locus
subspace. The scalar-restriction leaf supplies colon contraction and associated-
prime transport, and the principal-quotient leaf relates quotient-spectrum points
to minimal primes and exact annihilators. The finite-height leaves give well-founded
lower intervals in preorders and stabilization of antitone prime chains with their
all-index ideal infimum at a common index, using only finite initial height.
The principal-prime height leaf bounds height by one under well-founded strict divisibility,
with height one for regular prime generators and, in particular, prime elements in domains.
In unique factorization semirings of dimension at most two, every nonzero nonmaximal
prime is irreducible-principal, giving the zero, irreducible-principal or maximal classification.
Conversely, irreducible generators for all nonzero nonmaximal prime ideals bound the
Krull dimension of a commutative semiring by two, without unique factorization.
In dimension at most two, a nonmaximal prime ideal containing a prime non-zero-divisor
is generated by that same element. For every natural `n`, the closed-locus bound states
that in a commutative semiring of dimension at most `n + 1`, the canonical zero locus
of an ideal containing a non-zero-divisor has order dimension at most `n`, with its
inherited spectrum order and without properness or nonemptiness assumptions.
Without a finite ambient bound, the dimension of this same closed locus plus one
is at most `ringKrullDim R`, with addition in `WithBot ℕ∞` preserving bottom for
empty loci and including infinite dimensions.
The order comparison behind the dimension-two statement gives
reverse inequality from a nonminimal point to a nonmaximal point in any preorder.
For every natural bound `n`, the order dimension leaf characterizes dimension at most
`n + 1` by dimension at most `n` after deleting the original minimal points, and dually
for maximal points. Deleting both original boundaries gives the bound `n + 2` exactly
when the inherited interior has dimension at most `n`, without antisymmetry or inhabitation.
Without any finite bound, the nonminimal and nonmaximal loci each have dimension plus
one at most the ambient dimension; the original interior has dimension plus two at
most the ambient dimension. Addition is in `WithBot ℕ∞`, so an empty locus stays at
bottom after either finite shift, and infinite dimension is included.
The finite-product leaf classifies minimal primes over arbitrary ideals by labelled
coordinate minimal primes, and transports evaluation comaps at arbitrary index types.
The minimal-prime multiplier leaf clears an element of a minimal prime into the radical
by a multiplier outside that same prime, over any commutative semiring.
The integral-homomorphism leaf gives dimension equality for integral injections
without finiteness or nontriviality hypotheses. The minimal-prime localization leaf detects
minimality over an arbitrary ideal by equality of radicals after localization at a prime,
for commutative semirings and without a separate containment hypothesis.
The localization leaves characterize field localizations at arbitrary primes of
commutative semirings by minimality and target reducedness, and show that reducedness
is preserved by arbitrary localization.
The positive-power leaves show that contraction reflects containment in a radical
right-hand ideal when every target element has a positive power in the image, and
deduce injectivity of pullback on prime spectra of commutative semirings.
The product-semifield spectrum leaf upgrades the ultrafilter classification to a
homeomorphism for the existing Zariski and Stone topologies, with basis-transport laws
and density of coordinate primes. Ultrafilter zero-set ideals are maximal already
for division semiring fibres; for field fibres, the residue-field leaf identifies
their stock residue fields with dependent reduced products over the section ring,
compatibly with fibrewise field homomorphisms.
The maximal-spectrum leaves give continuous, closed pullback along integral ring
homomorphisms, with image the maximal ideals containing the kernel and surjectivity
for injective homomorphisms. They describe images of ideal zero-locus traces and retain
the pullback embedding along surjective ring homomorphisms as the same function.
The zero-dimensional spectrum leaf provides clopenness of basic opens, Hausdorffness
and the reduced, nontrivial, preconnected-spectrum field criterion.
The germ-regularity leaf characterizes intrinsic left, right and two-sided regularity
by eventual regularity of representatives, for arbitrary filters and codomains with
only multiplication.
The dependent-product pullback leaf reindexes arbitrary dependent types along
filter maps, with identity, composition and constant-germ agreement. Index equivalences
tending in both directions give equivalences using the same forward pullback.
The dependent-product nontriviality leaf characterizes nontriviality by existence
of a total section and frequent nontriviality of the fibres, without algebraic
structure or properness assumptions.
The ultrafilter no-zero-divisor leaves use only zero and multiplication: no zero
divisors in a dependent product is equivalent to eventual no-zero-divisor fibres,
with canonical monoid-with-zero and constant-germ specializations. The germ bridge
identifies the existing zero and multiplication for arbitrary filters.
The germ-reducedness leaf needs only zero and natural-number powers: reducedness is
equivalent to eventual reducedness of the value type, or to the filter being bottom
or the value type being reduced.
The monoid-germ leaf characterizes invertibility by the representative's filter-large
unit locus, for arbitrary filters and potentially noncommutative monoids.
The germ-map leaf characterizes injectivity of varying pointwise application by eventual
injectivity of the fibre maps, without properness or inhabitedness assumptions. Its pointed
fibre criterion needs only eventual preservation of the specified points for the converse.
The dependent product-map leaf gives the class-free canonical coordinate map, its
identity/composition/uniqueness and exact onto API, and selected-section reflection.
Its zero-only specialization uses the image of the same chosen zero section, without
installing another product zero instance. Exactness of two coordinate maps is
characterized by eventual exactness of the whole fibre pair, for arbitrary functions
with target zeros and a nonempty total source section type. The target zero is the
represented zero section. A target AddZeroClass bridge uses the existing named
additive product zero for the same arbitrary functions. In particular, given fibre
linear maps use the zero inherited from the given additive product record, with
source zeros supplying the section and no extra inhabitation hypothesis. The germ pointed
criterion is the constant-family bridge through the shared dependent section theorem.
The dependent-semimodule leaf equips additive reduced products with the binary quotient
action of the given varying fibre modules, over the existing scalar reduced product.
Its named data have representative and opaque-vector map laws and constant-germ
compatibility. Given fibre bases with one common finite index, the basis leaf
constructs the corresponding basis of this same semimodule. Its vector and coefficient
equations retain the given bases, and standard function bases recover the existing
finite-coordinate comparison. Equality of two represented scalar actions is equivalent
to eventual equality on every fibre vector, using only the given actions and vector zeros.
Whole-module annihilator membership and faithfulness of the given semimodule are
equivalent to their eventual fibre criteria. Faithfulness distinguishes two scalars
on all vectors and is not characterized by a zero annihilator at the semiring floor.
Torsion-freeness of this semimodule is equivalent to eventual fibre torsion-freeness,
by the coordinate-map injectivity and regular-scalar section criteria.
The germ-scalar leaf specializes zero-reflection to a selected zero-preserving action,
without scalar-zero or additive assumptions.
The non-zero-divisor leaf characterizes intrinsic left, right and two-sided
non-zero-divisors by their eventual coordinate loci, over arbitrary monoids with zero,
and gives the scalar submonoid criterion under an explicit eventual zero-preservation law.
The germ-center leaf characterizes the full intrinsic magma center by eventual
coordinate centrality, with a submonoid-center bridge requiring only a two-sided identity.
The matrix leaf constructs the ring equivalence for finite square matrices over
dependent reduced products, with the given semirings and stock matrix operations.
Representative and all-point entry equations characterize both directions, and
invertibility of a represented matrix is equivalent to the eventual unit locus of
those same fibre matrices.
The finite-product comparison is surjective for arbitrary coordinate types and is an
equivalence for finite coordinates, with representative, inverse and naturality laws.
Import a leaf directly for a smaller API.
-/
